Flag parent-element mismatch as type conflict

When two ConcreteTypes with the same TypeId get assigned to the same
equivalence class but their parent elements live in distinct classes,
Structure::close used to enqueue pairwise parent equalities, silently
collapsing the ambient parents. That is wrong: distinct ambient
parents must not be unified just because a type was imposed twice.

drain_equalities and impose_concrete_type now record a TypeConflict
in that case and leave the parents alone. Parents are only ever
compared by class, never enqueued for unification.

TypeConflict carries both ConcreteTypes (not just TypeIds) so callers
can inspect parent lists. conflict_to_error in algebra/mod.rs branches
on whether the TypeIds disagree: when they do it emits the existing
ConflictingTermType anchored on a term in el's class; when only the
parents disagree it emits a new ConflictingParentEl variant whose two
locations point at terms backing the innermost differing pair of
parent elements (last index in the parent list).
